    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Nuclear Factor Erythroid 2 Like (Nrf) transcription factors are a family of cap n’collar factors that regulate several metabolic pathways. Recent research has illustrated Nrf1, a member of this family, as the proteasomal subunit regulator and a potential target for cancer therapeutics. Nrf1 is an endoplasmic reticulum (ER)-bound protein in its glycosylated state, when it has glycosylated asparagine residues, a post-translational modification regulatory mechanism. In its deglycosylated state, however, it localizes to the cytosol or nucleus in the case of proteasomal inhibition. N-glycanase 1 (NGLY1) is the human cytosolic enzyme responsible for cleaving N-glycans (i.e. deglycosylation). \r\nNrf3 is another member of this family with significant homology to Nrf1, and presumably, comparable glycosylation regulation patterns. While Nrf3’s normal cellular function and target genes are unclear, research implicates Nrf3 in carcinogenesis, but Nrf3’s mechanistic roles in these pathways are unknown. Here we investigate NGLY1’s impact on Nrf3’s cellular processing, localization, and target gene expression. Previous research shows Nrf3 protein of differential molecular weights and subcellular localization dependent upon PNGase F (a bacterial glycan-cleaving amidase) activity, suggesting glycosylated and deglycosylated forms; however, NGLY1’s role as the cellular deglycosylating enzyme in Nrf3 processing and localization has not been established. By inhibiting NGLY1 in breast cancer cell lines (MDA-MB 231) and treating them with a proteasomal inhibitor, carfilzomib, we investigated changes in Nrf3’s subcellular localization in the presence or absence of deglycosylating activity and cellular stress. Additionally, by exposing Ngly1-/- human embryonic kidney (HEK) cells to carfilzomib, we examined Nrf3 processing under the similar conditions.\r\nWe find that Nrf3 cleavage from the ER and nuclear localization occur in the presence and absence of NGLY1 activity, but that NGLY1’s deglycosylating activity could be necessary for Nrf3’s activation. and eventual target gene regulation. This insight into Nrf3’s regulation presents Nrf3 activity as an additional substrate of NGLY1, providing potential therapeutic target considerations in relevant cancers.",
